Goal
Fix nested Flask Blueprint URL prefix resolution so that a child blueprint's 
constructor-level url_prefix is correctly combined with its parent blueprint's 
url_prefix, producing the expected concatenated route rule.

Files to modify
  • src/flask/blueprints.py

Implementation steps
  1. Step 1: In src/flask/blueprints.py, modify the 
Blueprint.register_blueprint() method. Before appending the (blueprint, options)
tuple to self._blueprints, check if options does not contain an explicit 
'url_prefix' but the blueprint argument has a non-None url_prefix attribute 
(from its constructor). If so, inject blueprint.url_prefix into the options 
dictionary.
  2. Step 2: If both the options contain a url_prefix (from explicit call) and 
the blueprint has its own url_prefix, ensure they are concatenated correctly: 
options['url_prefix'] should become the parent's prefix path + '/' + blueprint's
prefix path, normalizing slashes. Reference the existing _get_prefix_fragment 
helper or url prefix normalization logic in the same file if available.
  3. Step 3: Add or update a unit test in tests/test_blueprints.py that creates 
a parent Blueprint with url_prefix='/child', a child Blueprint with 
url_prefix='/parent', registers the child under the parent, registers the parent
under an app, and asserts that the endpoint rule is '/child/parent/' instead of 
'/'.

Tests to run
  $ pytest tests/test_blueprints.py -v

Risks
  ⚠  Changing how url_prefix is merged may affect existing users who relied on 
the buggy behavior of silently dropping the inner blueprint constructor prefix. 
We should ensure Flask's own url_prefix normalization (trailing slashes, leading
slashes) is preserved.

